{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 13, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—pink petals dusting the cobblestones like nature’s confetti. The city breathed quiet elegance: wooden teahouses, moss-covered temples, and the distant chime of temple bells. I sipped matcha in a centuries-old tea house, watching a geisha glide past in silk, her wooden geta clicking softly on stone.\n\nBy day three, I took the Shinkansen to Tokyo. The city pulsed—neon signs flickered above alleyways where ramen steam curled into the night. In Shibuya, I stood in the famous scramble crossing, swallowed by a sea of people, yet strangely at peace. I ate takoyaki from a street vendor who smiled and said, “Oishii?” I nodded, mouth full of octopus and batter.\n\nAt Meiji Shrine, I washed my hands in the purification fountain, bowed twice, clapped twice, and whispered a wish into the silence between the cedars. Later, I wandered Akihabara’s electric maze of anime figurines and retro game arcades, then escaped to the calm of Ueno Park, where pigeons pecked at bread near elderly men playing shogi.\n\nOne morning, I hiked up Mount Takao—hundreds of steps, mist clinging to the trees, monks chanting from a hidden temple. At the summit, the city sprawled below, hazy and beautiful. I ate an onigiri wrapped in nori, watching the clouds drift over skyscrapers and shrines in the same horizon.\n\nJapan doesn’t shout. It whispers: in the rustle of bamboo, the precision of a folded origami crane, the way a stranger bows without words. I came for the sights. I left with stillness.\n\nI didn’t bring back souvenirs. I brought back silence. And the taste of matcha on my tongue.\n\n— 298 words", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2209511399269104}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.09506416320800781}, {"token": " in", "logpro